Phase 5b: Correct manager-to-execution dependency direction
  Depends on the implementation checkpoint in 5a.
  Authority: Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md, sections The proposed boundary, Resource creation and adoption,
    and Backend dispatch and publication;
    Rendering.GpuCommands.Contracts.md, sections Authority and ownership and Resource references and factories.
  Outcome: the manager owns metadata/lifetime transactions without retrieving a concrete OpenGL backend.

  ☑ Design the internal creation, acquisition/query, capability and retirement service contracts and their
    composition. Explicitly trace public RHI request → backend execution → manager association/publication,
    including the manager's private initialization handoff. Keep native execution behind the backend;
    never route that handoff back through a public factory or recursively construct a resource owner.
  ☑ Replace GpuResourceManager calls through RHICommandContext.Current.OpenGL for buffer, texture,
    renderbuffer, sampler, view/buffer-texture, shader/program/program-pipeline, VAO, framebuffer, query,
    transform-feedback, synchronization, semaphore and memory-object creation and deletion.
    Supply required services through the agreed internal boundary rather than a backend service locator.
  ☑ Migrate buffer/texture/geometry and other manager-owned native acquisition/readers and capability
    lookups through the same defined service boundary. Queries remain manager-authorized; ordinary
    published metadata access must not acquire missing facts or introduce redundant native reads.
  ☑ Define manager-authorized context/configuration certification for imported engine geometry, consumed
    through the restricted integration contract in 5c. Keep native origin and vertex-configuration epoch
    checks behind that boundary; preserve original associations, borrow expiry and query-free reuse.
  ☑ Preserve the existing registry, canonical metadata, aliases/dependencies, exactly-once publication,
    provisional-identity cleanup and original-context retirement queues. Do not add another manager,
    dispatch registry, public recipient API or metadata authority to resolve the dependency cycle.
  ☑ Verify creation/failure, external acquisition, replacement and originating-context disposal through
    final services. Remove superseded concrete-backend access paths and their affected callers together;
    no forwarding shim, temporary service-locator property or duplicate dispatcher is permitted.

  Evidence: second review and independent stage-completion audit confirmed this dependency boundary.
    Manager initialization, acquisition/capabilities and retirement use the internal RHI execution
    contract; the registry/publication transaction and original-context queues remain singular.
    Final delegated Debug compilation: 0 errors/197 warnings; Release production: 0 errors/334 warnings.
    Focused tests: 364 passed, 15 inherited failures, 0 skipped (379 total); all failing names exactly
    match an isolated compiled checkpoint baseline. Additional registry/initialization/capability cases:
    53/53 passed. New manager-certification and corrected 4.5 initialization cases passed.
    Exact candidate, routes, baseline comparison and limitations are recorded in
